+# 🌉 Bridge Work Order — [P3] Browser extension distribution missing: supremeai-browser repo exists but there is no store presence (Monica Gap 7/8) (bridge-20261027-audit-gap-extension-store)
+
+## Problem
+The Bangla feature audit (2026-10-27) mapped browser extension distribution as Gap 7 (audit phase: 3). Verified: a dedicated `supremeai-browser` repository exists (read via the tower's GitHub App), and the platform already builds and ships one extension-shaped consumer — the VS Code/desktop path — on the shared `packages/shared-services` client. But there is no Chrome Web Store or Firefox Add-ons presence: no installable artifact, no store listing, no review funnel. Monica's distribution explicitly includes a browser extension surface with a large install base.
+
+The architecture makes this incremental rather than new: after the streaming client lands (PR #1455 replacing the 339-line non-streaming `SupremeAIService.ts`), the shared client already streams, retries with backoff, and surfaces typed errors. An MV3 extension popup that imports the same package gets the same behavior for free — no vendored copy, no drift.
+
+## Impact
+- Distribution: the browser is where the target users already live; an extension is the cheapest third surface after web (and before desktop polish).
+- Brand consistency: without a first-party extension, users installing look-alikes is a real risk in this category.
+- Engineering leverage: shared-services means the extension is mostly shell (popup UI + permissions), not another client to maintain.
+
+## Fix (smallest safe change)
+1. MV3 extension skeleton in `supremeai-browser`: popup chat surface + page-context menu actions (explain selection, summarize page).
+2. Import the chat client from `packages/shared-services` via the workspace protocol — zero vendored API code.
+3. Streaming popup chat against the existing gateway (SSE works in MV3 service-worker context via fetch streaming; the shared client's retry/timeout discipline carries over).
+4. CI builds the store zip (manifest v3, icons, permissions minimized: storage + active-tab only) so publishing is an operator upload, not a build project.
+5. Store listing draft: description, screenshots, and a permissions-rationale paragraph (review-pass likelihood).
+
+## Acceptance Criteria
+- [ ] Extension builds in CI and produces a store-ready zip artifact
+- [ ] Popup chat streams responses (visible incremental tokens, not wait-then-dump)
+- [ ] Page-context action (summarize selection) works on a normal https page
+- [ ] No vendored copy of the shared client — workspace import verified in the build graph
+- [ ] Permissions limited to storage + active-tab; listing draft includes rationale for each
+
+## Reflection Evidence (why + alternatives_rejected)
+**Why:** with the shared client landing, the marginal cost of a third surface is mostly shell; distribution breadth is the audit's phase-3 theme and the extension is its cheapest entry.
+**Alternatives rejected:** (a) iframe wrapper of the web app inside the popup — store policies and sandboxing make it fragile, and it would still need its own manifest anyway; (b) Firefox first — Chrome first by market share; the MV3 skeleton ports after review learnings; (c) deferring entirely — the repo exists and the shared client is landing now; the dependency that justified waiting is disappearing.
+
+## Labels
+`bridge:queued` `P3-low` `area:extension` `gap:monica-07`
